<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murano</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murano is a group of islands in the Venetian lagoon, about 1.5 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. In 1291 the republic ordered glassmakers to move their furnaces there, partly to reduce the risk of fire in the city, and Murano became the centre of Venetian glass-making, famous for its crystal glass, mirrors and coloured glass. Its glassworkers were long forbidden to leave the republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murano.jpg" alt="Murano" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wittylama · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murano_sunset.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Murano is made up of seven islands in the Venetian Lagoon, linked by bridges over eight channels. It measures about 1.5 kilometres across. It was once an independent comune but is now a frazione of the comune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. Its countryside was known for orchards and vegetable gardens until the nineteenth century, when more housing was built.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Murano was first settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and, from the sixth century, by people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altinum">Altinum</a> and Oderzo. At first it prospered as a fishing port and through the production of salt. From the eleventh century it began to decline as islanders moved to Dorsoduro. It had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> like that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, but from the thirteenth century it was governed by a podestà from Venice. Unlike the other islands of the lagoon, Murano minted its own coins.</p><p>Early in the second millennium hermits of the Camaldolese Order founded the Monastery of St Michael, which became a great centre of learning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. The cartographer Fra Mauro, whose maps were important to European exploration, was a monk there. French forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> suppressed it in 1810 and the grounds became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s major cemetery.</p><p>In 1291 all the glassmakers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> were required to move to Murano, since the Republic feared fire among the city's mostly wooden buildings. Exports began in the following century, at first of glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and mirrors. Aventurine glass was invented on the island, and for a while Murano was the main producer of glass in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By the fourteenth century glassmakers were allowed to wear swords, were immune from prosecution by the Venetian state, and saw their daughters marry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s wealthiest families, but they were forbidden to leave the Republic. Many took the risk and set up furnaces in other cities, sometimes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><p>Murano held a monopoly on high-quality glass for centuries and developed or refined optically clear glass, enamelled glass, aventurine, multicoloured millefiori, milk glass called lattimo and imitation gemstones. The Republic partly lost its monopoly at the end of the sixteenth century, when some glassmakers let the secrets be known in other European countries. Decline set in during the eighteenth century, yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glassmaking">glassmaking</a> is still the island's main industry. The Murano Glass Museum is housed in the Palazzo Giustinian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Industry flourished: shipbuilding at the Arsenal, glass-making, concentrated on <b>Murano</b> from 1291, silk weaving, soap, and from 1469 printing, with Aldus Manutius making Venice the leading centre of the European book trade around 1500.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Glass was made only on the island of <b>Murano</b>, to keep fires out of the city, and shipwrights built and armed the fleet in the state Arsenal; others made naval rope from imported hemp, tanned hides and spun wool on the Giudecca.</blockquote><a href="/societies/venice">Social Hierarchy of the Republic of Venice</a></li><li><blockquote>A Venetian law confined most of the city's glassmaking industry to the islands of <b>Murano</b>, in the lagoon north of Venice.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-8">8 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
